{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775344035-bpUX0NVhSK1CdewegNxE","role":"assistant","created":1775344056,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The issue is the regex - after the first month with year, subsequent months don't include the year. Let me extract the data differently - I'll look for rows with a specific pattern.\n\nLooking at the table content from my earlier output:\n```\n<tr><td>1984-Jan.</td><td>96</td><td>84</td>...\n<tr><td>Feb.</td><td>121</td><td>89</td>\n<tr><td>Mar.</td><td>124</td><td>63</td>\n```\n\nSo I need to track the year state and use it for subsequent rows without year prefix.\n\nActually, let me just manually verify and use the data I've already manually extracted. Based on my earlier analysis from reading the raw table output:\n- 1984: Jan=84, Feb=89, Mar=63, Apr=74, May=62, Jun=93, Jul=65, Aug=79, Sep=69, Oct=79, Nov=67, Dec=96\n- 1985: Jan=66, Feb=60, Mar=59, Apr=119, May=75, Jun=63, Jul=74, Aug=103, Sep=66, Oct=98, Nov=74, Dec=102\n- 1986: Jan=76, Feb=109, Mar=74, Apr=129, May=75, Jun=71, Jul=70, Aug=106, Sep=87, Oct=73, Nov=73, Dec=113\n- 1987: Jan=79, Feb=76, Mar=110\n\nThis gives us 39 months from Jan 1984 to Mar 1987. Let me now calculate the geometric mean and write the answer.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_f9188bbdcd0443e5886065e1","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me take a different approach - extract entire table and manually match\n\n# Read the first table from each file and display in readable format\n\ndef parse_table(content, start_year_pattern, end_year_pattern=None):\n    import re\n    tables = content.split('<table>')\n    \n    # Find the main data table (the one with monthly data)\n    for table in tables:\n        if 'The Judiciary' in table and 'Fiscal year' in table:\n            # Find the section with monthly data\n            rows = table.split('</tr>')\n            \n            # Look for data between start and end year patterns\n            capturing = False\n            current_year = None\n            values = {}\n            \n            for row in rows:\n                # Check for start of a year\n                if start_year_pattern in row:\n                    capturing = True\n                    current_year = start_year_pattern.split('-')[0]\n                \n                if end_year_pattern and end_year_pattern in row:\n                    capturing = False\n                    \n                if capturing and current_year:\n                    # Extract month and values\n                    # Look for the pattern: <td>Month.</td> or <td>Month</td>\n                    month_match = re.search(r'<td>(Jan|Feb|Mar|Apr|May|Jun|Jul|Aug|Sep|Oct|Nov|Dec)\\.?</td><td>(\\d+)</td><td>(\\d+)</td>', row)\n                    if month_match:\n                        month = month_match.group(1)\n                        judicial = month_match.group(3)\n                        values[f\"{current_year}-{month}\"] = int(judicial)\n                    \n                    # Also check for year-month pattern\n                    year_month_match = re.search(r'<td>(\\d{4})-(Jan|Feb|Mar|Apr|May|Jun|Jul|Aug|Sep|Oct|Nov|Dec)\\.?</td><td>(\\d+)</td><td>(\\d+)</td>', row)\n                    if year_month_match:\n                        year = year_month_match.group(1)\n                        month = year_month_match.group(2)\n                        judicial = year_month_match.group(4)\n                        values[f\"{year}-{month}\"] = int(judicial)\n                        \n            return values\n    \n    return {}\n\n# Try from 1985 bulletin for 1984\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1985_03_page_22.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Look for rows with \"1984-\" pattern\nimport re\n\n# Extract the section with 1984 monthly data\n# From what I saw, it goes from 1983-Dec through 1984-Dec\nrows = content.split('</tr>')\n\nprint(\"=== Extracting 1984 data manually ===\")\nfor row in rows:\n    if '1984-' in row and 'td>' in row:\n        # Find all numeric cells\n        nums = re.findall(r'<td>([\\d,]+)</td>', row)\n        if len(nums) >= 2:\n            # First numeric is Legislative, second is Judiciary\n            print(f\"Row: {row[:100]}...\")\n            print(f\"  Legislative: {nums[0]}, Judiciary: {nums[1]}\")\n            print()\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
